  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the storytelling contest?",
      "a": "The Host proposes that each pilgrim tell two tales on the way to Canterbury and two on the way back. The teller of the best tale wins a free supper at the Tabard, paid for by the others.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the prize in The Canterbury Tales?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does quiting mean in The Canterbury Tales?",
      "a": "To quite is to pay back or answer. Pilgrims answer each other's tales, as the Miller quites the Knight and the Reeve quites the Miller, turning the frame into a running quarrel.",
      "asks": [
        "What is quiting?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does Chaucer put himself among the pilgrims?",
      "a": "Chaucer the pilgrim is a naive narrator who admires everyone. The gap between his praise and the damning details creates the irony of the General Prologue.",
      "asks": [
        "Who is the narrator of The Canterbury Tales?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Did Chaucer finish The Canterbury Tales?",
      "a": "No. The plan implies about 120 tales, but only about 24 survive, some unfinished. Chaucer died in 1400 and the pilgrims never reach Canterbury in the text.",
      "asks": [
        "How many Canterbury Tales are there?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are the pilgrims going to Canterbury?",
      "a": "To visit the shrine of Saint Thomas Becket, the archbishop murdered in Canterbury Cathedral in 1170, which was one of the most popular pilgrimage sites in Europe.",
      "asks": [
        "Who is buried at Canterbury?"
      ]
    }
  ],
